{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788119531709,"sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a5a094f195bc271c","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/tir.rs","oldString":"    /// `reverse(v)`, `Vec<T> -> Vec<T>`: `v`'s elements in the opposite order. Blocking for the\n    /// same reason `sort` is (the whole Vec has to be there before the first output element\n    /// is), but unrestricted in element type, since reversing needs no comparison.\n    Reverse,\n}","newString":"    /// `reverse(v)`, `Vec<T> -> Vec<T>`: `v`'s elements in the opposite order. Blocking for the\n    /// same reason `sort` is (the whole Vec has to be there before the first output element\n    /// is), but unrestricted in element type, since reversing needs no comparison.\n    Reverse,\n    /// `sum(v)`, the reduction of `+` over `v`'s elements, at the element type's own width: a\n    /// `Vec<Int>` sums to `Int`, a `Vec<Int64>` to `Int64`, and an empty Vec sums to 0. Each\n    /// addition wraps the way the language's `+` does, so a sum that leaves the width is the\n    /// same number a hand-written fold would produce (kantord/toylang#140).\n    Sum,\n    /// `max(v)`, the greatest of `v`'s elements, `Opt<T>` because an empty Vec has no maximum\n    /// -- the same answer indexing gives to absence (kantord/toylang#140). Restricted to the\n    /// element types whose `<` orders already, so a backend can reach for its native maximum.\n    Max,\n}"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/tir.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/tir.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/tir.rs\n@@ -232,8 +232,17 @@\n     /// `reverse(v)`, `Vec<T> -> Vec<T>`: `v`'s elements in the opposite order. Blocking for the\n     /// same reason `sort` is (the whole Vec has to be there before the first output element\n     /// is), but unrestricted in element type, since reversing needs no comparison.\n     Reverse,\n+    /// `sum(v)`, the reduction of `+` over `v`'s elements, at the element type's own width: a\n+    /// `Vec<Int>` sums to `Int`, a `Vec<Int64>` to `Int64`, and an empty Vec sums to 0. Each\n+    /// addition wraps the way the language's `+` does, so a sum that leaves the width is the\n+    /// same number a hand-written fold would produce (kantord/toylang#140).\n+    Sum,\n+    /// `max(v)`, the greatest of `v`'s elements, `Opt<T>` because an empty Vec has no maximum\n+    /// -- the same answer indexing gives to absence (kantord/toylang#140). Restricted to the\n+    /// element types whose `<` orders already, so a backend can reach for its native maximum.\n+    Max,\n }\n \n pub struct Func {\n     pub name: String,\n","filediff":{"file":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/tir.rs","patch":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/tir.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/tir.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/tir.rs\n@@ -232,8 +232,17 @@\n     /// `reverse(v)`, `Vec<T> -> Vec<T>`: `v`'s elements in the opposite order. Blocking for the\n     /// same reason `sort` is (the whole Vec has to be there before the first output element\n     /// is), but unrestricted in element type, since reversing needs no comparison.\n     Reverse,\n+    /// `sum(v)`, the reduction of `+` over `v`'s elements, at the element type's own width: a\n+    /// `Vec<Int>` sums to `Int`, a `Vec<Int64>` to `Int64`, and an empty Vec sums to 0. Each\n+    /// addition wraps the way the language's `+` does, so a sum that leaves the width is the\n+    /// same number a hand-written fold would produce (kantord/toylang#140).\n+    Sum,\n+    /// `max(v)`, the greatest of `v`'s elements, `Opt<T>` because an empty Vec has no maximum\n+    /// -- the same answer indexing gives to absence (kantord/toylang#140). Restricted to the\n+    /// element types whose `<` orders already, so a backend can reach for its native maximum.\n+    Max,\n }\n \n pub struct Func {\n     pub name: String,\n","additions":9,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"src/tir.rs","time":{"start":1788119531704,"end":1788119531707}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Good. Now let me start editing. First tir.rs — add Sum and Max to the Builtin enum.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0543aa97e001xwHvau377z2I4Z","sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","messageID":"msg_0543aa4c8001EcW211Gjzjxi5a"}}
