{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788119632315,"sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b1612458a9b618af","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_lua.rs","limit":20,"offset":490},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_lua.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n490: #[derive(Default)]\n491: struct Helpers {\n492:     select: bool,\n493:     field: bool,\n494:     index: bool,\n495:     unwrap: bool,\n496:     arith: bool,\n497:     arith64: bool,\n498:     map: bool,\n499:     range: bool,\n500:     collect: bool,\n501:     jsonlines: bool,\n502:     tail: bool,\n503:     flatten: bool,\n504:     chars: bool,\n505:     sort: bool,\n506:     reverse: bool,\n507:     eq: bool,\n508: }\n509: \n\n(Showing lines 490-509 of 952. Use offset=510 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"#[derive(Default)]\nstruct Helpers {\n    select: bool,\n    field: bool,\n    index: bool,\n    unwrap: bool,\n    arith: bool,\n    arith64: bool,\n    map: bool,\n    range: bool,\n    collect: bool,\n    jsonlines: bool,\n    tail: bool,\n    flatten: bool,\n    chars: bool,\n    sort: bool,\n    reverse: bool,\n    eq: bool,\n}\n","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_lua.rs","text":"#[derive(Default)]\nstruct Helpers {\n    select: bool,\n    field: bool,\n    index: bool,\n    unwrap: bool,\n    arith: bool,\n    arith64: bool,\n    map: bool,\n    range: bool,\n    collect: bool,\n    jsonlines: bool,\n    tail: bool,\n    flatten: bool,\n    chars: bool,\n    sort: bool,\n    reverse: bool,\n    eq: bool,\n}\n","lineStart":490,"lineEnd":509,"totalLines":952,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/emit_lua.rs","time":{"start":1788119632303,"end":1788119632312}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now Lua. I need to:\n1. Add SUM_HELPER and MAX_HELPER constants.\n2. Add `sum` and `max` fields to the `Helpers` struct.\n3. Update `builtin_helpers`.\n4. Update the emit table.\n5. Add the Builtin match arms.\n\nFor Lua, Int is 64-bit integers with tl_i32 narrowing for Int arithmetic. So sum helper needs to branch on whether to narrow. I'll make the helper take a narrowing flag... Actually, simpler: the emit can pass the elem type. Let me make the helper generic over Int/Int64 via a flag parameter. Hmm, but Lua is dynamically typed — I can have one helper that takes a boolean `narrow`.\n\nActually, wait — for Int, the helper narrows each step with tl_i32. For Int64, no narrowing. Let me write:\n\n```lua\nlocal function tl_sum(v, narrow)\n  local acc = 0\n  for i = 1, #v do\n    acc = acc + v[i]\n    if narrow then acc = tl_i32(acc) end\n  end\n  return acc\nend\n```\n\nThis uses `tl_i32` which is defined in ARITH_HELPER. But ARITH_HELPER is only included when `used.arith`. Hmm — so the sum helper would need tl_i32 present even when no other arithmetic is used. That's a dependency I need to handle: including SUM_HELPER should also pull in ARITH_HELPER (which defines tl_i32). \n\nAlternatively, I can inline the narrowing in the sum helper to avoid the dependency:\n```lua\nlocal function tl_sum(v, narrow)\n  local acc = 0\n  for i = 1, #v do\n    acc = acc + v[i]\n    if narrow then acc = ((acc + 2147483648) % 4294967296) - 2147483648 end\n  end\n  return acc\nend\n```\nHmm, that duplicates tl_i32's logic. Better to reuse. But reusing means I must ensure ARITH_HELPER is emitted whenever SUM_HELPER is. That's a helper-to-helper edge the code already states (\"Helper-to-helper dependencies are stated rather than read back\" — in Go and Lua). \n\nActually wait — simpler: I could decide that `sum` of `Vec<Int>` narrows only via the emit side — i.e., in the Lua emit, I emit `tl_sum(arg, elem == Int)`. And make tl_sum depend on tl_i32 (ARITH_HELPER). In the `used_helpers` walk, when I see `Builtin::Sum`, I set `used.sum = true` and if the elem is Int I also set `used.arith = true` (so ARITH_HELPER with tl_i32 is included).\n\nWait, but the ARITH_HELPER in Lua defines tl_i32, tl_div, tl_rem. Including it for sum is fine.\n\nLet me reconsider — maybe simpler to have the helper do the narrowing inline with a tiny comment, avoiding the cross-helper dependency. Actually, reusing tl_i32 is cleaner and the dependency statement is a well-established pattern here. Let me go with:\n\n- `used.sum |= which == Builtin::Sum;` and `used.arith |= which == Builtin::Sum && elem is Int`.\n\nHmm, but `builtin_helpers(which, used)` doesn't have access to the arg type. Let me check the signature: `fn builtin_helpers(which: Builtin, used: &mut Helpers)`. I can change it or handle the arith dependency in the walk. Looking at the walk: `Kind::Builtin { which, arg } => { builtin_helpers(*which, used); walk(arg, used); }`. The `arg.ty.elem()` is available there. Let me handle the arith dependency in the walk.\n\nActually — let me simplify by making tl_sum self-contained with inline narrowing. The narrowing is one line and duplicated from tl_i32. Actually the \"comment the why\" rule... a comment explaining the inline narrowing would say \"the same wrap tl_i32 does\". Hmm. I think reusing tl_i32 is genuinely cleaner. Let me handle the dependency in the walk.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the whole design. For Lua, maybe I don't need a helper at all — I could emit the sum inline as a Lua expression... no, Lua has no fold expression. A helper is the right call.\n\nLet me now also think about max for Lua:\n```lua\nlocal function tl_max(v)\n  if #v == 0 then return \"none\" end\n  local m = v[1]\n  for i = 2, #v do\n    if v[i] > m then m = v[i] end\n  end\n  return { some = m }\nend\n```\n\nFor Lua, Int and Int64 are both 64-bit integers. Comparing raw values is correct for both (Int values are already narrowed i32 sign-extended). Good.\n\nLet me write these helpers. For the sum helper, I'll make the emit pass the narrow flag:\n\nActually, let me reconsider — instead of a runtime boolean flag, I could emit two different helper invocations based on the static elem type. The Lua emitter knows `arg.ty.elem()`. So:\n- Int: `tl_sum(v, true)` \n- Int64: `tl_sum(v, false)`\n\nAnd in the walk, set `used.arith = true` when a Sum over Int appears (so tl_i32 exists).\n\nLet me implement. First the Helpers struct.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0543c49a5001JbAndUAC0y7AFj","sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","messageID":"msg_0543bf09b001ZGNugSyjQODVdI"}}
