{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788119826787,"sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8fb3631a67f9bcfa","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_py.rs","newString":"const JSONLINES_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_jsonlines(v, f):\n    return \"\\n\".join(f(e) for e in v)\n\"#;\n\n/// Python's integers are unbounded, so both widths wrap by emulation -- `tl_i32` for Int and\n/// `tl_i64` for Int64, the same two helpers arithmetic already uses.\nconst SUM_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_sum(v):\n    acc = 0\n    for x in v:\n        acc = tl_i32(acc + x)\n    return acc\n\n\ndef tl_sum64(v):\n    acc = 0\n    for x in v:\n        acc = tl_i64(acc + x)\n    return acc\n\"#;\n\nconst MAX_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_max(v):\n    if len(v) == 0:\n        return \"none\"\n    return {\"some\": max(v)}\n\"#;","oldString":"const JSONLINES_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_jsonlines(v, f):\n    return \"\\n\".join(f(e) for e in v)\n\"#;"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_py.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_py.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_py.rs\n@@ -132,8 +132,30 @@\n const JSONLINES_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_jsonlines(v, f):\n     return \"\\n\".join(f(e) for e in v)\n \"#;\n \n+/// Python's integers are unbounded, so both widths wrap by emulation -- `tl_i32` for Int and\n+/// `tl_i64` for Int64, the same two helpers arithmetic already uses.\n+const SUM_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_sum(v):\n+    acc = 0\n+    for x in v:\n+        acc = tl_i32(acc + x)\n+    return acc\n+\n+\n+def tl_sum64(v):\n+    acc = 0\n+    for x in v:\n+        acc = tl_i64(acc + x)\n+    return acc\n+\"#;\n+\n+const MAX_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_max(v):\n+    if len(v) == 0:\n+        return \"none\"\n+    return {\"some\": max(v)}\n+\"#;\n+\n /// Iterating characters rather than bytes, which agrees with the C runtime's byte loop because\n /// the two differ only above U+007F, where both pass the value through unchanged.\n const QUOTE_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_quote(s):\n     out = ['\"']\n","filediff":{"file":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_py.rs","patch":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_py.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_py.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_py.rs\n@@ -132,8 +132,30 @@\n const JSONLINES_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_jsonlines(v, f):\n     return \"\\n\".join(f(e) for e in v)\n \"#;\n \n+/// Python's integers are unbounded, so both widths wrap by emulation -- `tl_i32` for Int and\n+/// `tl_i64` for Int64, the same two helpers arithmetic already uses.\n+const SUM_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_sum(v):\n+    acc = 0\n+    for x in v:\n+        acc = tl_i32(acc + x)\n+    return acc\n+\n+\n+def tl_sum64(v):\n+    acc = 0\n+    for x in v:\n+        acc = tl_i64(acc + x)\n+    return acc\n+\"#;\n+\n+const MAX_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_max(v):\n+    if len(v) == 0:\n+        return \"none\"\n+    return {\"some\": max(v)}\n+\"#;\n+\n /// Iterating characters rather than bytes, which agrees with the C runtime's byte loop because\n /// the two differ only above U+007F, where both pass the value through unchanged.\n const QUOTE_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_quote(s):\n     out = ['\"']\n","additions":22,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"src/emit_py.rs","time":{"start":1788119826779,"end":1788119826785}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now Python. Python helpers are textually gated too (`uses(...)` on decls). Let me add:\n- `tl_sum(v, narrow)` — Python integers are unbounded, so Int (32-bit) needs `tl_i32` narrowing per step; Int64 needs `tl_i64` narrowing per step. Both are one-modulo wraps. I could pass a flag or branch on element type. Let me write:\n```python\ndef tl_sum(v, wrap):\n    acc = 0\n    for x in v:\n        acc = acc + x\n        if wrap == 32:\n            acc = tl_i32(acc)\n        elif wrap == 64:\n            acc = tl_i64(acc)\n    return acc\n```\nHmm, that's clunky. Better two functions like JS:\n```python\ndef tl_sum(v):\n    acc = 0\n    for x in v:\n        acc = tl_i32(acc + x)\n    return acc\n\ndef tl_sum64(v):\n    acc = 0\n    for x in v:\n        acc = tl_i64(acc + x)\n    return acc\n```\nThese need tl_i32 (I32_HELPER) and tl_i64 (I64_HELPER). The Python emit's helper table includes `(arith || uses(\"tl_i32(\"), I32_HELPER)` and `(arith64 || uses(\"tl_i64(\"), I64_HELPER)`. So if tl_sum is emitted, `uses(\"tl_i32(\")` is true (since tl_sum body references tl_i32), which pulls in I32_HELPER. \n\nFor max:\n```python\ndef tl_max(v):\n    if len(v) == 0:\n        return \"none\"\n    return {\"some\": max(v)}\n```\nPython's `max` works on a list of numbers (int), including Int64 (unbounded ints). Since Python ints are one type, one helper works for both.\n\nNow, the helpers table in Python reads `uses()` from decls. So I add entries:\n```\n(uses(\"tl_sum(\") || uses(\"tl_sum64(\"), SUM_HELPER),\n(uses(\"tl_max(\"), MAX_HELPER),\n```\n\nAnd the Builtin match:\n```rust\nBuiltin::Sum => {\n    if tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int) {\n        format!(\"tl_sum({})\", expr(enums, arg))\n    } else {\n        format!(\"tl_sum64({})\", expr(enums, arg))\n    }\n}\nBuiltin::Max => format!(\"tl_max({})\", expr(enums, arg)),\n```\n\nWait — the `uses` closure in Python is defined as `let uses = |name: &str| decls.contains(name);` and it reads the `decls` which contains the program body. But the helper text itself (SUM_HELPER) is added to `out` (not decls), so `uses(\"tl_i32(\")` would only be true if the *program* uses tl_i32. Hmm wait — let me re-read.\n\nIn Python emit:\n```rust\nlet mut decls = printers(program);\n// program funcs...\n// body...\n// THEN:\nlet uses = |name: &str| decls.contains(name);\nlet unwrap = uses(\"tl_unwrap(\");\n...\nfor (on, text) in [ (arith || uses(\"tl_i32(\"), I32_HELPER), ... ]\n```\n\nSo `uses()` checks the `decls` string which is the emitted program (functions + main), NOT the helpers. So `uses(\"tl_i32(\")` is true if the program body calls tl_i32 (i.e., uses `+` arithmetic on Int). For `tl_sum`, which is a helper that internally calls tl_i32 — `uses(\"tl_i32(\")` in the program decls is false unless the program itself uses arithmetic. So the I32_HELPER wouldn't be included just because tl_sum needs it!\n\nThis is a real dependency issue. In Python, helper-to-helper edges \"are still stated rather than read\". The I32_HELPER is gated on `arith || uses(\"tl_i32(\")`. If a program only calls `sum([1,2])`, decls contains `tl_sum(...)` but not `tl_i32(...)`. So `arith` is false (no tl_div/tl_rem), and `uses(\"tl_i32(\")` is false. So I32_HELPER is NOT included → tl_sum's reference to tl_i32 fails.\n\nSo I need to make the SUM_HELPER inclusion also pull in I32/I64 helpers. The cleanest: gate I32_HELPER and I64_HELPER on the sum helpers too. Let me change:\n```\n(arith || uses(\"tl_i32(\") || uses(\"tl_sum(\"), I32_HELPER),\n(arith64 || uses(\"tl_i64(\") || uses(\"tl_sum64(\"), I64_HELPER),\n```\n\nHmm wait — but `uses(\"tl_sum(\")` checks decls, and decls contains `tl_sum(...)` when the program calls sum over Int. Good. But what about `uses(\"tl_i32(\")` for a program that only does `sum([1,2])`? decls would contain `tl_sum([1,2])` but not `tl_i32(`, so `uses(\"tl_i32(\")` is false, but `uses(\"tl_sum(\")` is true → I32_HELPER included. \n\nSimilarly for sum64.\n\nActually, wait — let me reconsider. The I32_HELPER is also needed by tl_sum directly. Rather than complicate the gating, I could inline the wrap in the sum helpers:\n```python\ndef tl_sum(v):\n    acc = 0\n    for x in v:\n        acc = ((acc + x + 2147483648) % 4294967296) - 2147483648\n    return acc\n```\nThat duplicates tl_i32 logic. Not great.\n\nBetter: update the I32/I64 gates to also trigger on the sum helpers. Let me do:\n```\n(arith || uses(\"tl_i32(\") || uses(\"tl_sum(\"), I32_HELPER),\n(arith64 || uses(\"tl_i64(\") || uses(\"tl_sum64(\"), I64_HELPER),\n```\n\nThis is the \"helper-to-helper edges are stated rather than read\" pattern. Good.\n\nLet me write the Python helpers and updates.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0543f20f40019sjLua0Ny3iB64","sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","messageID":"msg_0543eabe6001emOWos3wnDKw7D"}}
