{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788119852493,"sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-b8e9ddef273def51","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_rs.rs","newString":"const REVERSE_HELPER: &str = r#\"fn tl_reverse<T: Clone>(v: &[T]) -> Vec<T> {\n    let mut out = v.to_vec();\n    out.reverse();\n    out\n}\n\"#;\n\n// `wrapping_add` is the same wrap `+` gets through INT_HELPER/ARITH_HELPER, so a fold is the\n// repeated addition with nothing else to spell.\nconst SUM_HELPER: &str = r#\"fn tl_sum(v: &[i32]) -> i32 {\n    v.iter().fold(0i32, |acc, x| acc.wrapping_add(*x))\n}\n\nfn tl_sum64(v: &[i64]) -> i64 {\n    v.iter().fold(0i64, |acc, x| acc.wrapping_add(*x))\n}\n\"#;\n\n// `Option::max` is exactly the empty-is-absent answer, and `Ord` covers both integer widths.\nconst MAX_HELPER: &str = r#\"fn tl_max<T: Clone + Ord>(v: &[T]) -> Option<T> {\n    v.iter().cloned().max()\n}\n\"#;","oldString":"const REVERSE_HELPER: &str = r#\"fn tl_reverse<T: Clone>(v: &[T]) -> Vec<T> {\n    let mut out = v.to_vec();\n    out.reverse();\n    out\n}\n\"#;"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_rs.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_rs.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_rs.rs\n@@ -122,8 +122,25 @@\n     out\n }\n \"#;\n \n+// `wrapping_add` is the same wrap `+` gets through INT_HELPER/ARITH_HELPER, so a fold is the\n+// repeated addition with nothing else to spell.\n+const SUM_HELPER: &str = r#\"fn tl_sum(v: &[i32]) -> i32 {\n+    v.iter().fold(0i32, |acc, x| acc.wrapping_add(*x))\n+}\n+\n+fn tl_sum64(v: &[i64]) -> i64 {\n+    v.iter().fold(0i64, |acc, x| acc.wrapping_add(*x))\n+}\n+\"#;\n+\n+// `Option::max` is exactly the empty-is-absent answer, and `Ord` covers both integer widths.\n+const MAX_HELPER: &str = r#\"fn tl_max<T: Clone + Ord>(v: &[T]) -> Option<T> {\n+    v.iter().cloned().max()\n+}\n+\"#;\n+\n const RANGE_HELPER: &str = r#\"fn tl_range(n: i32) -> Vec<i32> {\n     (0..n.max(0)).collect()\n }\n \"#;\n","filediff":{"file":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_rs.rs","patch":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_rs.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_rs.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_rs.rs\n@@ -122,8 +122,25 @@\n     out\n }\n \"#;\n \n+// `wrapping_add` is the same wrap `+` gets through INT_HELPER/ARITH_HELPER, so a fold is the\n+// repeated addition with nothing else to spell.\n+const SUM_HELPER: &str = r#\"fn tl_sum(v: &[i32]) -> i32 {\n+    v.iter().fold(0i32, |acc, x| acc.wrapping_add(*x))\n+}\n+\n+fn tl_sum64(v: &[i64]) -> i64 {\n+    v.iter().fold(0i64, |acc, x| acc.wrapping_add(*x))\n+}\n+\"#;\n+\n+// `Option::max` is exactly the empty-is-absent answer, and `Ord` covers both integer widths.\n+const MAX_HELPER: &str = r#\"fn tl_max<T: Clone + Ord>(v: &[T]) -> Option<T> {\n+    v.iter().cloned().max()\n+}\n+\"#;\n+\n const RANGE_HELPER: &str = r#\"fn tl_range(n: i32) -> Vec<i32> {\n     (0..n.max(0)).collect()\n }\n \"#;\n","additions":17,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"src/emit_rs.rs","time":{"start":1788119852490,"end":1788119852492}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now Rust. Rust has `wrapping_add` for i32/i64. Helpers:\n```rust\nfn tl_sum(v: &[i32]) -> i32 {\n    let mut acc = 0i32;\n    for x in v {\n        acc = acc.wrapping_add(*x);\n    }\n    acc\n}\nfn tl_sum64(v: &[i64]) -> i64 {\n    let mut acc = 0i64;\n    for x in v {\n        acc = acc.wrapping_add(*x);\n    }\n    acc\n}\nfn tl_max<T: Clone + Ord>(v: &[T]) -> Option<T> {\n    v.iter().cloned().max()\n}\n```\nActually `tl_max` can use `v.iter().cloned().max()` which returns None for empty. Clean.\n\nRust gating: `uses(\"tl_tail(\")` etc. Let me check the Rust emit table and the fail gating. Rust's `fail` gate includes `uses(\"tl_at(\") || uses(\"tl_tail(\")...`. tl_max uses no tl_fail. tl_sum uses no tl_fail. Good.\n\nRust helper entries:\n```\n(uses(\"tl_sum(\") || uses(\"tl_sum64(\"), SUM_HELPER),\n(uses(\"tl_max(\"), MAX_HELPER),\n```\n\nAnd Builtin match:\n```rust\nBuiltin::Sum => {\n    if tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int) {\n        format!(\"tl_sum(&{})\", self.expr(arg))\n    } else {\n        format!(\"tl_sum64(&{})\", self.expr(arg))\n    }\n}\nBuiltin::Max => format!(\"tl_max(&{})\", self.expr(arg)),\n```\n\nNote Rust uses `&{}` for vec args (tl_tail(&{}), tl_sort(&{})).","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0543f8732001vCdo9ENBgnlt44","sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","messageID":"msg_0543f7306001TKMgNEdYRHs370"}}
