 in\")?;\n1367:                 let slot_ty = self.llvm_type(&t.ty)?;\n1368:                 let slot = self\n1369:                     .builder\n1370:                     .build_alloca(slot_ty, \"cond\")\n1371:                     .map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;\n1372: \n1373:                 let test = self.expr(cond)?.into_int_value();\n1374:                 let yes = self.ctx.append_basic_block(function, \"then\");\n1375:                 let no = self.ctx.append_basic_block(function, \"else\");\n1376:                 let done = self.ctx.append_basic_block(function, \"cond.done\");\n1377:                 self.builder\n1378:                     .build_conditional_branch(test, yes, no)\n1379:                     .map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;\n\n(Showing lines 1290-1379 of 2562. Use offset=1380 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"                                slot.into(),\n                            ],\n                            \"\",\n                        )\n                        .map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;\n                }\n                rec.into()\n            }\n\n            Kind::EnumLit { variant, payload } => {\n                if t.ty.as_opt().is_some() {\n                    return self.opt_lit(payload.as_deref());\n                }\n                self.enum_lit(&t.ty, variant, payload.as_deref())?\n            }\n\n            Kind::VecLit(items) => {\n                let elem =\n                    t.ty.elem()\n                        .ok_or_else(|| \"a Vec literal that is not a Vec\".to_string())?","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_llvm.rs","text":"                                slot.into(),\n                            ],\n                            \"\",\n                        )\n                        .map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;\n                }\n                rec.into()\n            }\n\n            Kind::EnumLit { variant, payload } => {\n                if t.ty.as_opt().is_some() {\n                    return self.opt_lit(payload.as_deref());\n                }\n                self.enum_lit(&t.ty, variant, payload.as_deref())?\n            }\n\n            Kind::VecLit(items) => {\n                let elem =\n                    t.ty.elem()\n                        .ok_or_else(|| \"a Vec literal that is not a Vec\".to_string())?\n                        .clone();\n                self.vec_lit(items, &elem)?\n            }\n\n            Kind::Select {\n                source,\n                param,\n                pred,\n            } => self.select(source, *param, pred)?,\n\n            Kind::Map {\n                source,\n                param,\n                body,\n            } => self.map(source, *param, body, &t.ty)?,\n\n            // The stream, materialized eagerly: whatever consumes it -- `collect`, a mapper --\n            // works on the Vec of its entries.\n            Kind::Lines => self.call_rt(self.rt.collect_lines, &[], \"lines\")?,\n\n            Kind::Input => {\n                let global = self\n                    .input_slot\n                    .ok_or_else(|| \"an `input` node the module never read stdin for\".to_string())?;\n                let slot = self\n                    .builder\n                    .build_load(self.ctx.i64_type(), global, \"input_slot\")\n                    .map_err(|e| e.to_string())?\n                    .into_int_value();\n                self.read_slot(slot, &t.ty)?\n            }\n\n            // Already a proper Vec pointer, unlike Input's raw slot: tl_read_inputs assembles\n            // it itself, so there is nothing here for read_slot to unpack.\n            Kind::Inputs => {\n                let elem =\n                    crate::tir::runtime_elem(&t.ty).expect(\"checked to be Vec<T> or Stream<T>\");\n                let descriptor = self.string_const(&descriptor(self.enums, elem));\n                self.call_rt(self.rt.read_inputs, &[descriptor.into()], \"inputs\")?\n            }\n\n            Kind::Field { base, name } => self.field(base, name, &t.ty)?,\n\n            // The runtime returns the unwrapped slot as a pointer, so a scalar comes back\n            // needing the integer put back; a pointer-shaped value is already right.\n            // A real branch rather than a select, so only the taken side runs: either branch\n            // may allocate, loop, or divide by zero.\n            Kind::Cond {\n                cond,\n                then,\n                otherwise,\n            } => {\n                let function = self\n                    .builder\n                    .get_insert_block()\n                    .and_then(|b| b.get_parent())\n                    .ok_or(\"no function to branch in\")?;\n                let slot_ty = self.llvm_type(&t.ty)?;\n                let slot = self\n                    .builder\n                    .build_alloca(slot_ty, \"cond\")\n                    .map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;\n\n                let test = self.expr(cond)?.into_int_value();\n                let yes = self.ctx.append_basic_block(function, \"then\");\n                let no = self.ctx.append_basic_block(function, \"else\");\n                let done = self.ctx.append_basic_block(function, \"cond.done\");\n                self.builder\n                    .build_conditional_branch(test, yes, no)\n                    .map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;","lineStart":1290,"lineEnd":1379,"totalLines":2562,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/emit_llvm.rs","time":{"start":1788119919743,"end":1788119919768}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now the native (LLVM) backend and the C runtime. This is the trickiest.\n\nC runtime functions:\n```c\n/* `sum` over a Vec whose element is Int or Int64, one column of raw int64 slots. `narrow`\n * distinguishes the widths: Int's additions are brought back to 32 bits the way `+` is, and\n * Int64's left alone. Accumulated unsigned so an overflow is defined rather than UB. */\nint64_t tl_vec_sum(const tl_vec *v, int narrow) {\n    uint64_t acc = 0;\n    for (int64_t i = 0; i < v->len; i++) {\n        acc += (uint64_t)v->cols[0][i];\n        if (narrow) {\n            acc = (uint64_t)(int64_t)(int32_t)acc;\n        }\n    }\n    return (int64_t)acc;\n}\n\n/* `max` over the same two element types: the greatest raw slot, NULL on an empty Vec (the same\n * absence encoding tl_opt_some uses everywhere else). */\nint64_t *tl_vec_max(const tl_vec *v) {\n    if (v->len == 0) {\n        return NULL;\n    }\n    int64_t m = v->cols[0][0];\n    for (int64_t i = 1; i < v->len; i++) {\n        if (v->cols[0][i] > m) {\n            m = v->cols[0][i];\n        }\n    }\n    return tl_opt_some(m);\n}\n```\n\nWait — for the narrow case: `acc` is uint64_t. `(int64_t)(int32_t)acc` — casting uint64_t directly to int32_t is implementation-defined for values out of range, but in practice gcc/clang truncate the low 32 bits then sign-extend... actually `(int32_t)acc` where acc is uint64_t truncates modulo 2^32 to int32_t (implementation-defined but all real compilers do two's complement truncation). Then cast to int64_t sign-extends. That's the standard wrap. The runtime already uses similar techniques? Let me check how the C runtime handles i32 wrap — it doesn't (all in IR). Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. To be safe and well-defined, I can compute in unsigned and normalize:\n```c\nif (narrow) {\n    acc = (uint32_t)acc;  // truncate to low 32 bits (defined for unsigned)\n}\nreturn (int64_t)acc;\n```\nWait, `(uint32_t)acc` truncates acc to low 32 bits, giving a value 0..2^32-1. Then `(int64_t)acc` gives 0..2^32-1 (positive). But an Int value can be negative — the slot holds a sign-extended i32. When we truncate the accumulated uint64 to uint32 and then cast to int64_t, we get a positive number in 0..2^32-1, NOT the signed representation.\n\nExample: sum = -1 in two's complement i32. Slot after narrowing should be 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF (sign-extended -1). If I do `acc = (uint32_t)acc` → 0xFFFFFFFF (4294967295), then `return (int64_t)acc` → 4294967295, not -1. Wrong.\n\nSo I need sign extension after truncation. `(int64_t)(int32_t)(uint32_t)acc`:\n- `(uint32_t)acc` → low 32 bits, 0..2^32-1.\n- `(int32_t)(uint32_t)` → signed reinterpretation, -2^31..2^31-1. This cast from uint32_t to int32_t is implementation-defined for values > INT32_MAX, but on all real compilers (gcc, clang, msvc) it's two's complement reinterpretation, which is what we want.\n- `(int64_t)(int32_t)` → sign-extends. Defined.\n\nSo `acc = (uint64_t)(int64_t)(int32_t)(uint32_t)acc;` — this gives the sign-extended i32 value. That's the standard trick. It's well-defined except for the uint32_t→int32_t step which is implementation-defined but universally two's complement in practice.\n\nHmm, but the C runtime is compiled with `cc` and might be compiled with `-fwrapv`? Let me check the link flags. Actually the `link` function just runs `cc <object> <runtime> -o <out>`. No special flags. Let me check whether the existing runtime relies on any wrap. Actually, since the existing runtime doesn't do Int arithmetic, this is new territory.\n\nActually, simpler and fully well-defined: since each element is already a sign-extended i32 (values in i32 range), and Int `+` narrows per-step, I can keep acc as int64_t and use the fact that intermediate acc + element stays within... no, acc can exceed i32 range before narrowing.\n\nLet me reconsider — actually there's an even cleaner approach avoiding C UB concerns: do the whole sum in IR. But that's a lot of code. \n\nAlternatively — I realize the `narrow` approach in C, with `(uint64_t)(int64_t)(int32_t)(uint32_t)`, is the standard idiom and fine. The existing codebase — let me double check whether the C runtime already has a narrowing or wrap helper... No.\n\nActually wait — hold on. Let me reconsider whether per-step narrowing for Int is even the right semantics, or whether sum should just compute in the natural width and wrap once at the end. \n\nHmm. Actually, let me think about what \"correct\" means. In the language, `+` on Int wraps at 32-bit. `sum(v)` is \"the reduction of `+`\". A reduction applies `+` repeatedly, so each application wraps. Per-step narrowing is the faithful reading. The plan says nothing about overflow. The most defensible implementation is per-step, matching repeated `+`, so all backends agree. I'll keep per-step.\n\nOK so for the C runtime, I'll use the truncate-and-sign-extend idiom. Let me double check the C is compiled without warnings as errors — the `cc` call: `cc object runtime -o out`. If there are warnings, it still links (warnings aren't errors unless -Werror). But the runtime has a lot of code; adding functions won't add new warnings if written cleanly.\n\nNow, for the LLVM side:\n- Add fields `vec_sum` and `vec_max` to Runtime struct.\n- Declare them in `Runtime::new`:\n  - `vec_sum`: `i64t.fn_type(&[ptr.into(), i64t.into()], false)` — (vec*, i64 narrow) -> i64. Wait, should narrow be an i1 or i64? The C function takes `int narrow`. I'll pass an i64 0/1.\n  - `vec_max`: `ptr.fn_type(&[ptr.into()], false)` — (vec*) -> ptr (tl_opt_some result is a pointer to a slot).\n- In the Builtin match:\n  - `Builtin::Sum`: compute narrow flag: `elem == Int`. Call `rt.vec_sum(&[arg, narrow_const], \"sum\")`. The result is an i64 (Int or Int64 value in a slot — the raw value). For Int, the value is already narrowed by the C function. For Int64, raw. The result type of the Tir node is Int or Int64. But here's the catch — in LLVM, Int is represented as i64 but \"narrowed after every operation\". The C function returns a value already in Int range (sign-extended i32 in an i64), which is exactly how Int values are stored in slots. So no further narrowing needed. \n  - `Builtin::Max`: result is Opt<T> — a pointer. Call `rt.vec_max(&[arg], \"max\")`. The node's type is `Opt<Int>` or `Opt<Int64>`. How does the LLVM backend represent Opt? Via tl_opt_some → a pointer (i64*). Let me check how Index (which returns Opt) is emitted in LLVM. `Kind::Index` — let me look.\n\nLet me look at how Index and opt values flow in the LLVM emitter to make sure my max emission matches the Opt representation.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05440aa19001E8eV6TXXtX6Pq2","sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","messageID":"msg_0544007180012efrI6k48RNpsy"}}
