{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788119975749,"sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-bdd9278dbe21b97a","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_llvm.rs","newString":"    chars: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_sort_int: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_sort_str: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_reverse: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_sum: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_max: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n}","oldString":"    chars: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_sort_int: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_sort_str: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n    vec_reverse: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n}"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_llvm.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_llvm.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_llvm.rs\n@@ -75,8 +75,10 @@\n     chars: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n     vec_sort_int: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n     vec_sort_str: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n     vec_reverse: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n+    vec_sum: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n+    vec_max: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n }\n \n /// What a compiler-introduced binding holds.\n ///\n","filediff":{"file":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_llvm.rs","patch":"Index: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_llvm.rs\n===================================================================\n--- /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_llvm.rs\n+++ /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_llvm.rs\n@@ -75,8 +75,10 @@\n     chars: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n     vec_sort_int: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n     vec_sort_str: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n     vec_reverse: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n+    vec_sum: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n+    vec_max: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n }\n \n /// What a compiler-introduced binding holds.\n ///\n","additions":2,"deletions":0},"truncated":false},"title":"src/emit_llvm.rs","time":{"start":1788119975744,"end":1788119975746}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"LLVM `llvm_type` for `Opt<T>` (a `Type::Enum` with `as_opt()`) is just a pointer. So my `Max` result (Opt<Int> or Opt<Int64>) is a pointer, which `tl_vec_max` returns directly (int64_t *). \n\nAnd for the `Unwrap` of Max — `Kind::Unwrap` calls `tl_unwrap(o, depth)` which takes a pointer and returns a pointer, then reads the slot for scalar inner types. So Max flows naturally into unwrap.\n\nNow let me write the C runtime functions. For sum, I want to handle the narrow flag. But wait — I need to double check the C compile won't complain about the `(int64_t)(int32_t)` cast — that's fine, C allows explicit casts.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: can I avoid the `narrow` parameter in C by always operating at 64-bit (no narrowing) in C and... no — Int sum must wrap at 32 bits to agree with all other backends.\n\nLet me write:\n```c\n/* `sum` over a Vec of Int or Int64 (kantord/toylang#140): the reduction of `+`, per-addition\n * the way the language's own `+` wraps. Both widths live in the same i64 slot, so `narrow`\n * is what tells Int (32-bit wrap after every addition) from Int64 (none). Accumulated in\n * uint64_t so no addition can overflow into undefined behaviour; the narrow step truncates\n * to 32 bits and sign-extends. */\nint64_t tl_vec_sum(const tl_vec *v, int narrow) {\n    uint64_t acc = 0;\n    for (int64_t i = 0; i < v->len; i++) {\n        acc += (uint64_t)v->cols[0][i];\n        if (narrow) {\n            acc = (uint64_t)(int64_t)(int32_t)acc;\n        }\n    }\n    return (int64_t)acc;\n}\n```\n\nHmm wait, the truncation for narrow: `(int32_t)acc` where acc is uint64_t → implementation-defined (but universally low-32-bits two's complement in practice) then `(int64_t)` sign-extends. Good.\n\nHmm, actually wait. There's a subtle issue: `acc` is uint64_t. When narrow is false (Int64), `return (int64_t)acc` — acc accumulates mod 2^64 (unsigned wraps defined). Casting uint64_t to int64_t is implementation-defined but universally two's complement reinterpretation. Good.\n\n```c\n/* `max` over a Vec of Int or Int64: the greatest raw slot. NULL on an empty Vec, the same\n * absence encoding tl_opt_some uses everywhere else, so a caller unwraps it the way it\n * unwraps an Index result. */\nint64_t *tl_vec_max(const tl_vec *v) {\n    if (v->len == 0) {\n        return NULL;\n    }\n    int64_t m = v->cols[0][0];\n    for (int64_t i = 1; i < v->len; i++) {\n        if (v->cols[0][i] > m) {\n            m = v->cols[0][i];\n        }\n    }\n    return tl_opt_some(m);\n}\n```\n\nNote `tl_opt_some` is defined above in the file (line 1026). Both these functions go after `tl_vec_reverse` (line ~1141), which is after tl_opt_some. Good.\n\nNow, in the Runtime struct:\n```rust\nvec_sum: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\nvec_max: FunctionValue<'ctx>,\n```\nDeclarations:\n```rust\nvec_sum: module.add_function(\n    \"tl_vec_sum\",\n    i64t.fn_type(&[ptr.into(), i64t.into()], false),\n    None,\n),\nvec_max: module.add_function(\n    \"tl_vec_max\",\n    ptr.fn_type(&[ptr.into()], false),\n    None,\n),\n```\n\nAnd the Builtin match:\n```rust\nBuiltin::Sum => {\n    let narrow = tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty) == Some(&Type::Int);\n    let narrow = self.ctx.i64_type().const_int(narrow as u64, false);\n    self.call_rt(self.rt.vec_sum, &[arg, narrow.into()], \"sum\")?\n}\nBuiltin::Max => self.call_rt(self.rt.vec_max, &[arg], \"max\")?,\n```\n\nWait — `call_rt` takes `&[BasicValueEnum]`. `arg` is a `BasicValueEnum` (the expr result). `narrow.into()` converts IntValue into BasicValueEnum. Good.\n\nBut hold on — the arg here is the pointer to the Vec (for Vec<Int>, llvm_type is a pointer). The C function takes `const tl_vec *v`. Good.\n\nNow, one concern: in the `Kind::Builtin` arm of the LLVM emitter, `arg` is already computed via `self.expr(arg)?`. Let me look at the exact context again (line 1411-1417):\n```rust\nKind::Builtin { which, arg } => {\n    let elem_ty = crate::tir::runtime_elem(&arg.ty).cloned();\n    let record_ty = arg.ty.clone();\n    let arg = self.expr(arg)?;\n    match which { ... }\n}\n```\n\nSo inside the match, `arg` is the computed value (BasicValueEnum), and `arg.ty` was moved — wait, `arg` shadowed. The original `arg: &Tir` was shadowed by `arg: BasicValueEnum`. But `elem_ty` and `record_ty` were captured before. So in my Sum arm I can use `elem_ty` to check Int. Let me use `elem_ty`:\n```rust\nBuiltin::Sum => {\n    let narrow = self.ctx.i64_type().const_int(\n        (elem_ty.as_ref() == Some(&Type::Int)) as u64,\n        false,\n    );\n    self.call_rt(self.rt.vec_sum, &[arg, narrow.into()], \"sum\")?\n}\n```\n\nWait, `elem_ty` is `Option<Type>` (cloned). `elem_ty.as_ref() == Some(&Type::Int)`. Good.\n\nNow let me write these changes. First the Runtime struct and declarations.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_054418142001p4tYb9GuV7aWw5","sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","messageID":"msg_054413c7b001oK9cP2QD06k9qa"}}
