{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788120067033,"sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8ea65909a4811a8a","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_jq.rs","limit":80,"offset":1},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_jq.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The jq backend.\n2: //!\n3: //! The other three targets are imperative and this one is not, so it is the only backend where\n4: //! the compiler has to say what it means in stream terms. That makes it a check on the\n5: //! relationship to jq rather than another way to run a program: everything the design diverges\n6: //! on has to be bridged here explicitly.\n7: //!\n8: //! Two mappings are worth naming. A dimension spec becomes `.[]` plus a reification, since\n9: //! keeping a dimension in a stream language means iterating and collecting. And `Opt` carries\n10: //! its enum's own runtime shape even here -- `{\"some\": v}` present, `\"none\"` absent -- tagged\n11: //! in memory like every backend now, with `null` appearing only when the printer flattens the\n12: //! tags away. One consequence is load-bearing: no in-memory value is ever JSON null, so jq's\n13: //! own null (an out-of-range `.[i]`) still unambiguously means \"was not there\".\n14: \n15: use crate::ast::BinOp;\n16: use crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Kind, LocalId, Program, Tir};\n17: use crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n18: \n19: /// The binding stdin is read into. jq puts the input in `.`, which this backend needs for the\n20: /// subject, so it is bound away before the program starts.\n21: const INPUT: &str = \"$t_input\";\n22: const INPUTS: &str = \"$t_inputs\";\n23: \n24: /// jq has no integer type, so 32-bit wrapping is arithmetic on doubles. Addition and\n25: /// subtraction stay exact because the result never passes 2^33; multiplication does not, since\n26: /// the true product needs 62 bits, so it goes through 16-bit halves whose partial products each\n27: /// stay under 2^53. Verified against C and Math.imul, including -2147483648 * -1.\n28: const ARITH_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_i32: . as $r\n29:   | ((($r % 4294967296) + 4294967296) % 4294967296)\n30:   | if . >= 2147483648 then . - 4294967296 else . end;\n31: def tl_mul($a; $b):\n32:   ($a | tl_i32) as $x | ($b | tl_i32) as $y\n33:   | (if $x < 0 then $x + 4294967296 else $x end) as $ua\n34:   | (if $y < 0 then $y + 4294967296 else $y end) as $ub\n35:   | ($ua / 65536 | floor) as $ah | ($ua % 65536) as $al\n36:   | ($ub / 65536 | floor) as $bh | ($ub % 65536) as $bl\n37:   | (($al * $bl) + ((($ah * $bl) + ($al * $bh)) % 65536) * 65536) | tl_i32;\n38: def tl_div($a; $b):\n39:   if $b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\")\n40:   else ($a / $b | trunc) | tl_i32 end;\n41: def tl_rem($a; $b):\n42:   if $b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\") else ($a % $b) | tl_i32 end;\n43: \"#;\n44: \n45: /// Int64 arithmetic on doubles, exact only within +/-2^53 (kantord/toylang#83). That boundary\n46: /// is documented rather than papered over: 32-bit wrapping could be emulated exactly through\n47: /// 16-bit halves because every partial product fit a double, and no such split reassembles a\n48: /// 64-bit product or a mod-2^64 wrap from pieces a double can hold. So `+`, `-` and `*` are\n49: /// jq's own, `%` casts through C's 64-bit integers inside jq itself, and a program whose\n50: /// Int64 values stay within 2^53 -- every corpus case does -- agrees with the other six\n51: /// backends; one that leaves it diverges here, and docs/reference/types/int64.md says so.\n52: const ARITH64_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_div64($a; $b):\n53:   if $b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\") else ($a / $b | trunc) end;\n54: def tl_rem64($a; $b):\n55:   if $b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\") else ($a % $b) end;\n56: \"#;\n57: \n58: pub fn emit(program: &Program) -> Result<String, String> {\n59:     let enums = &program.enums;\n60:     let mut out = String::new();\n61:     let (arith, arith64) = uses_arith(program);\n62:     if arith {\n63:         out.push_str(ARITH_HELPER);\n64:     }\n65:     if arith64 {\n66:         out.push_str(ARITH64_HELPER);\n67:     }\n68: \n69:     out.push_str(&printers(program)?);\n70: \n71:     // jq resolves a `def` only against what is already defined, so definitions have to come out\n72:     // callee-first. The checker collects every signature before checking any body and therefore\n73:     // accepts a call to a function defined further down, which is a rule this target does not\n74:     // share. Lua needed forward declarations for the same reason; jq has no way to write one.\n75:     for f in ordered(program)? {\n76:         // A unary function's argument arrives as `.` and is bound before the body runs; a\n77:         // nullary one ignores `.` entirely, since it has nothing to bind.\n78:         out.push_str(&match &f.param {\n79:             Some(param) => format!(\n80:                 \"def {}: . as ${} | {};\\n\",\n\n(Showing lines 1-80 of 873. Use offset=81 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The jq backend.\n//!\n//! The other three targets are imperative and this one is not, so it is the only backend where\n//! the compiler has to say what it means in stream terms. That makes it a check on the\n//! relationship to jq rather than another way to run a program: everything the design diverges\n//! on has to be bridged here explicitly.\n//!\n//! Two mappings are worth naming. A dimension spec becomes `.[]` plus a reification, since\n//! keeping a dimension in a stream language means iterating and collecting. And `Opt` carries\n//! its enum's own runtime shape even here -- `{\"some\": v}` present, `\"none\"` absent -- tagged\n//! in memory like every backend now, with `null` appearing only when the printer flattens the\n//! tags away. One consequence is load-bearing: no in-memory value is ever JSON null, so jq's\n//! own null (an out-of-range `.[i]`) still unambiguously means \"was not there\".\n\nuse crate::ast::BinOp;\nuse crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Kind, LocalId, Program, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n\n/// The binding stdin is read into. jq puts the input in `.`, which this backend needs for the\n/// subject, so it is bound away before the program starts.","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_jq.rs","text":"//! The jq backend.\n//!\n//! The other three targets are imperative and this one is not, so it is the only backend where\n//! the compiler has to say what it means in stream terms. That makes it a check on the\n//! relationship to jq rather than another way to run a program: everything the design diverges\n//! on has to be bridged here explicitly.\n//!\n//! Two mappings are worth naming. A dimension spec becomes `.[]` plus a reification, since\n//! keeping a dimension in a stream language means iterating and collecting. And `Opt` carries\n//! its enum's own runtime shape even here -- `{\"some\": v}` present, `\"none\"` absent -- tagged\n//! in memory like every backend now, with `null` appearing only when the printer flattens the\n//! tags away. One consequence is load-bearing: no in-memory value is ever JSON null, so jq's\n//! own null (an out-of-range `.[i]`) still unambiguously means \"was not there\".\n\nuse crate::ast::BinOp;\nuse crate::tir::{self, Builtin, Kind, LocalId, Program, Tir};\nuse crate::ty::{self, Enums, Type};\n\n/// The binding stdin is read into. jq puts the input in `.`, which this backend needs for the\n/// subject, so it is bound away before the program starts.\nconst INPUT: &str = \"$t_input\";\nconst INPUTS: &str = \"$t_inputs\";\n\n/// jq has no integer type, so 32-bit wrapping is arithmetic on doubles. Addition and\n/// subtraction stay exact because the result never passes 2^33; multiplication does not, since\n/// the true product needs 62 bits, so it goes through 16-bit halves whose partial products each\n/// stay under 2^53. Verified against C and Math.imul, including -2147483648 * -1.\nconst ARITH_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_i32: . as $r\n  | ((($r % 4294967296) + 4294967296) % 4294967296)\n  | if . >= 2147483648 then . - 4294967296 else . end;\ndef tl_mul($a; $b):\n  ($a | tl_i32) as $x | ($b | tl_i32) as $y\n  | (if $x < 0 then $x + 4294967296 else $x end) as $ua\n  | (if $y < 0 then $y + 4294967296 else $y end) as $ub\n  | ($ua / 65536 | floor) as $ah | ($ua % 65536) as $al\n  | ($ub / 65536 | floor) as $bh | ($ub % 65536) as $bl\n  | (($al * $bl) + ((($ah * $bl) + ($al * $bh)) % 65536) * 65536) | tl_i32;\ndef tl_div($a; $b):\n  if $b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\")\n  else ($a / $b | trunc) | tl_i32 end;\ndef tl_rem($a; $b):\n  if $b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\") else ($a % $b) | tl_i32 end;\n\"#;\n\n/// Int64 arithmetic on doubles, exact only within +/-2^53 (kantord/toylang#83). That boundary\n/// is documented rather than papered over: 32-bit wrapping could be emulated exactly through\n/// 16-bit halves because every partial product fit a double, and no such split reassembles a\n/// 64-bit product or a mod-2^64 wrap from pieces a double can hold. So `+`, `-` and `*` are\n/// jq's own, `%` casts through C's 64-bit integers inside jq itself, and a program whose\n/// Int64 values stay within 2^53 -- every corpus case does -- agrees with the other six\n/// backends; one that leaves it diverges here, and docs/reference/types/int64.md says so.\nconst ARITH64_HELPER: &str = r#\"def tl_div64($a; $b):\n  if $b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\") else ($a / $b | trunc) end;\ndef tl_rem64($a; $b):\n  if $b == 0 then error(\"toylang: divided by zero\") else ($a % $b) end;\n\"#;\n\npub fn emit(program: &Program) -> Result<String, String> {\n    let enums = &program.enums;\n    let mut out = String::new();\n    let (arith, arith64) = uses_arith(program);\n    if arith {\n        out.push_str(ARITH_HELPER);\n    }\n    if arith64 {\n        out.push_str(ARITH64_HELPER);\n    }\n\n    out.push_str(&printers(program)?);\n\n    // jq resolves a `def` only against what is already defined, so definitions have to come out\n    // callee-first. The checker collects every signature before checking any body and therefore\n    // accepts a call to a function defined further down, which is a rule this target does not\n    // share. Lua needed forward declarations for the same reason; jq has no way to write one.\n    for f in ordered(program)? {\n        // A unary function's argument arrives as `.` and is bound before the body runs; a\n        // nullary one ignores `.` entirely, since it has nothing to bind.\n        out.push_str(&match &f.param {\n            Some(param) => format!(\n                \"def {}: . as ${} | {};\\n\",","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":80,"totalLines":873,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/emit_jq.rs","time":{"start":1788120066999,"end":1788120067028}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now the jq backend. Let me add sum and max. For jq:\n- Sum: element type is Int or Int64. For Int, wrap each addition with tl_i32. For Int64, no wrap.\n  - `({} | reduce .[] as $x (0; ((. + $x) | tl_i32)))` for Int\n  - `({} | reduce .[] as $x (0; . + $x))` for Int64\n  Wait — I need to check how `tl_i32` is defined/used in jq. Let me check the jq emit for Int arithmetic — `arith` uses `tl_i32` for Int. Is `tl_i32` a helper function in jq? jq allows function definitions at the top. Let me look at the top of emit_jq.rs.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05442e9690013tPUWG3KNi2Teg","sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","messageID":"msg_05442cf950019f3LOazVamOwr3"}}
