\\\"none\\\" else {{some: max}} end)\",\n    expr(enums, arg)\n),\n```\n\nWait — careful with jq syntax: `if length == 0 then \"none\" else {some: max} end`. Inside the else, `max` is a filter applied to `.` which is the array. `{some: max}` — `max` here is a bare filter that computes the max of `.`. That works: `{some: max}` produces `{\"some\": <max>}`. Good.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety with jq's `if` on a filter: `(ARG | if length == 0 then \"none\" else {some: max} end)`. Since ARG produces one value (a Vec), this is fine.\n\nNow, one more consideration for jq sum over Int: each iteration `((. + $x) | tl_i32)`. Since $x is already an Int (i32 range, but in jq it's a double), and `.` is the accumulator. Good.\n\nLet me now also double check: the `reduce .[] as $x (0; ...)` — jq's reduce syntax. Yes: `reduce .[] as $x (0; . + $x)`.\n\nNow let me write the jq changes.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_054435e55001DTdvDZcqEYsFjR","sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","messageID":"msg_054430f29001hpRqZ14FXFcor2"}}
