{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788120175324,"sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8a2315b78c758934","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cargo run --quiet -- run /tmp/tl_sum_prog.toy lua 2>/dev/null"},"output":"(no output)","metadata":{"output":"(no output)","exit":1,"truncated":false},"title":"cargo run --quiet -- run /tmp/tl_sum_prog.toy lua 2>/dev/null","time":{"start":1788120175258,"end":1788120175323}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I see the bug: SUM_HELPER is emitted before ARITH_HELPER. In Lua, `local function tl_sum` references `tl_i32`, which is a global at call time (Lua resolves globals at call time), but `tl_i32` is defined after `tl_sum` in the emitted file. Since `tl_i32` is defined as a `local function` AFTER `tl_sum`'s definition, when `tl_sum` runs, `tl_i32` IS a local in scope (locals are visible after their definition). The order: `tl_sum` defined first, then `tl_i32` defined. When `tl_sum` body executes later, `tl_i32` is in scope. That's fine!\n\nBut the error was \"attempt to call a nil value (global 'tl_i32')\". So `tl_i32` was NOT defined at all in the earlier run. But in this emit output, ARITH_HELPER IS present (tl_i32 defined). So why did the earlier run fail?\n\nWait — the earlier run output shows the Lua with tl_i32 present now. But the run failed. Let me re-run now. Maybe the build I ran earlier was before my `used.arith` fix was compiled? No — I added `used.arith` logic before the build. Hmm wait, actually let me re-check the order in the earlier failing run. The emit output shows tl_sum first, then tl_i32. That should work at runtime (tl_i32 is a local in scope).\n\nOh wait — actually there's a subtle Lua scoping issue. `local function tl_sum` is a syntactic form. Inside `tl_sum`, the reference to `tl_i32` — at the time `tl_sum` is *defined*, `tl_i32` is not yet a local (it's declared after). But Lua closures capture locals by reference to the scope; when `tl_sum` is *called* later, `tl_i32` is a local in the enclosing scope (defined by then). So it should work.\n\nLet me actually re-run.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_054448c0a001WwyO7qk1EH7ACq","sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","messageID":"msg_054446cc4001Ejhd2dDK7zLYZY"}}
