{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788120228034,"sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-9396cce8498225c2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_lua.rs","limit":30,"offset":255},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_lua.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n255: \n256: pub fn emit(program: &Program) -> String {\n257:     let enums = &program.enums;\n258:     let mut out = String::new();\n259: \n260:     let used = used_helpers(program);\n261:     // A top-level Str prints raw, the way jq's -r does; anything else prints as JSON. So a\n262:     // string inside a Vec is quoted while a bare string is not.\n263:     let structured = program.body.ty != Type::Str;\n264:     let quote = (structured && needs_quote(&program.body.ty)) || used.jsonlines;\n265:     let join = (structured && contains_vec(enums, &program.body.ty)) || used.jsonlines;\n266:     for (on, text) in [\n267:         (used.select, SELECT_HELPER),\n268:         (used.field, FIELD_HELPER),\n269:         (quote, QUOTE_HELPER),\n270:         (join, JOIN_HELPER),\n271:         (used.index, OPT_HELPER),\n272:         (used.unwrap, UNWRAP_HELPER),\n273:         (used.tail, TAIL_HELPER),\n274:         (used.flatten, FLATTEN_HELPER),\n275:         (used.sort, SORT_HELPER),\n276:         (used.reverse, REVERSE_HELPER),\n277:         (used.sum, SUM_HELPER),\n278:         (used.max, MAX_HELPER),\n279:         (used.arith, ARITH_HELPER),\n280:         (used.arith64, ARITH64_HELPER),\n281:         (used.map, MAP_HELPER),\n282:         (used.range, RANGE_HELPER),\n283:         (used.collect, COLLECT_HELPER),\n284:         (used.jsonlines, JSONLINES_HELPER),\n\n(Showing lines 255-284 of 993. Use offset=285 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"\npub fn emit(program: &Program) -> String {\n    let enums = &program.enums;\n    let mut out = String::new();\n\n    let used = used_helpers(program);\n    // A top-level Str prints raw, the way jq's -r does; anything else prints as JSON. So a\n    // string inside a Vec is quoted while a bare string is not.\n    let structured = program.body.ty != Type::Str;\n    let quote = (structured && needs_quote(&program.body.ty)) || used.jsonlines;\n    let join = (structured && contains_vec(enums, &program.body.ty)) || used.jsonlines;\n    for (on, text) in [\n        (used.select, SELECT_HELPER),\n        (used.field, FIELD_HELPER),\n        (quote, QUOTE_HELPER),\n        (join, JOIN_HELPER),\n        (used.index, OPT_HELPER),\n        (used.unwrap, UNWRAP_HELPER),\n        (used.tail, TAIL_HELPER),\n        (used.flatten, FLATTEN_HELPER),","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/src/emit_lua.rs","text":"\npub fn emit(program: &Program) -> String {\n    let enums = &program.enums;\n    let mut out = String::new();\n\n    let used = used_helpers(program);\n    // A top-level Str prints raw, the way jq's -r does; anything else prints as JSON. So a\n    // string inside a Vec is quoted while a bare string is not.\n    let structured = program.body.ty != Type::Str;\n    let quote = (structured && needs_quote(&program.body.ty)) || used.jsonlines;\n    let join = (structured && contains_vec(enums, &program.body.ty)) || used.jsonlines;\n    for (on, text) in [\n        (used.select, SELECT_HELPER),\n        (used.field, FIELD_HELPER),\n        (quote, QUOTE_HELPER),\n        (join, JOIN_HELPER),\n        (used.index, OPT_HELPER),\n        (used.unwrap, UNWRAP_HELPER),\n        (used.tail, TAIL_HELPER),\n        (used.flatten, FLATTEN_HELPER),\n        (used.sort, SORT_HELPER),\n        (used.reverse, REVERSE_HELPER),\n        (used.sum, SUM_HELPER),\n        (used.max, MAX_HELPER),\n        (used.arith, ARITH_HELPER),\n        (used.arith64, ARITH64_HELPER),\n        (used.map, MAP_HELPER),\n        (used.range, RANGE_HELPER),\n        (used.collect, COLLECT_HELPER),\n        (used.jsonlines, JSONLINES_HELPER),","lineStart":255,"lineEnd":284,"totalLines":993,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/emit_lua.rs","time":{"start":1788120228025,"end":1788120228032}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The emit shows tl_i32 is defined at line 13 and tl_sum references it at line 5. That should work... unless the `run` path runs a different emitted source. \n\nWait — the `run` path calls `run_on(src, None, Backend::Lua)`. In `run_on`:\n```rust\nBackend::Lua => run_lua(\n    &emit_lua::emit(&program),\n    ...\n)\n```\nSame source.\n\nHmm, but the error is at runtime: \"attempt to call a nil value (global 'tl_i32')\" inside tl_sum. In the emitted Lua, `tl_i32` IS a local. If it's a local, why \"global 'tl_i32'\"?\n\nOH. I see it now! In Lua, `local function tl_sum` is being defined. Inside, `tl_i32(acc)` refers to the *global* `tl_i32`, NOT the local one. Why? Because `local function tl_sum` captures the lexical scope at definition time. At the point `tl_sum` is defined (line 1), the local `tl_i32` (line 13) is NOT YET DECLARED in the enclosing scope. So when Lua compiles `tl_sum`'s body, `tl_i32` is not a lexically visible local — it resolves to the *global* `tl_i32`. Even though `tl_i32` is defined later as a local in the same chunk, `tl_sum`'s body was already compiled to reference the global.\n\nYes! That's the classic Lua gotcha: a local declared later in the same chunk is not visible to an earlier function definition. `local function tl_sum` is equivalent to `local tl_sum; tl_sum = function(...)` — the body references `tl_i32` as a global because the local `tl_i32` declaration comes later.\n\nSo the SUM_HELPER must come AFTER ARITH_HELPER in the emitted output. The emit table order matters. ARITH_HELPER is emitted before SUM_HELPER in the table (arith is listed earlier), but in the OUTPUT, SUM_HELPER came first! \n\nLooking at the emit output: line 1 is tl_sum, line 13 is tl_i32. So SUM_HELPER was emitted BEFORE ARITH_HELPER. But in the table, `(used.arith, ARITH_HELPER)` comes before `(used.sum, SUM_HELPER)`. Why did SUM come first?\n\nOH WAIT. Let me look at the Lua emit table again — I need to check the order. Earlier I read:\n```rust\n(used.tail, TAIL_HELPER),\n(used.flatten, FLATTEN_HELPER),\n(used.sort, SORT_HELPER),\n(used.reverse, REVERSE_HELPER),\n(used.sum, SUM_HELPER),\n(used.max, MAX_HELPER),\n(used.arith, ARITH_HELPER),\n...\n```\n\nSo SUM_HELPER (index 8) comes before ARITH_HELPER (index 11). In the output, SUM_HELPER's text is emitted first, then ARITH_HELPER. So `tl_sum` is defined before `tl_i32` → the global-reference problem.\n\nFix: move SUM_HELPER emission AFTER ARITH_HELPER. OR reorder so ARITH comes before SUM. The cleanest is to place `(used.arith, ARITH_HELPER)` and `(used.arith64, ARITH64_HELPER)` before the sum entries. Let me reorder the table.\n\nActually, the table order determines output order. I'll move sum/max entries to after the arith entries. Let me look at the current table and reorder.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_054455a43001EJSZE5gFbvB9Ih","sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","messageID":"msg_05445237c001zj0cl16wJagBSV"}}
