{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788120263963,"sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a4de623b44176d89","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"printf 'fn big() -> Int64 = 9223372036854775807\\n\\nsum([big(), i64(1)])\\n' > /tmp/tl_wrap64.toy; for b in lua js jq go py native rust; do printf \"%s wrap64=\" \"$b\"; cargo run --quiet -- run /tmp/tl_wrap64.toy \"$b\" 2>&1 | rg -v \"warning|never used|-->|^\\s*\\||^\\s*[0-9]+ \\||^\\s*=|^\\s*\\^|help:|note:\" | tail -1; done"},"output":"lua wrap64=-9223372036854775808\njs wrap64=-9223372036854775808\njq wrap64=9223372036854776000\ngo wrap64=-9223372036854775808\npy wrap64=-9223372036854775808\nnative wrap64=-9223372036854775808\nrust wrap64=-9223372036854775808\n","metadata":{"output":"lua wrap64=-9223372036854775808\njs wrap64=-9223372036854775808\njq wrap64=9223372036854776000\ngo wrap64=-9223372036854775808\npy wrap64=-9223372036854775808\nnative wrap64=-9223372036854775808\nrust wrap64=-9223372036854775808\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"printf 'fn big() -> Int64 = 9223372036854775807\\n\\nsum([big(), i64(1)])\\n' > /tmp/tl_wrap64.toy; for b in lua js jq go py native rust; do printf \"%s wrap64=\" \"$b\"; cargo run --quiet -- run /tmp/tl_wrap64.toy \"$b\" 2>&1 | rg -v \"warning|never used|-->|^\\s*\\||^\\s*[0-9]+ \\||^\\s*=|^\\s*\\^|help:|note:\" | tail -1; done","time":{"start":1788120263959,"end":1788120263961}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Interesting — the wrap32 case agrees everywhere (-2147483648), but the wrap64 case produced NO output on any backend. That means those programs failed. The issue: `wide(9223372036854775807)` — but `i64(x)` takes an `Int`, and `9223372036854775807` doesn't fit in an Int (32-bit). So the literal `9223372036854775807` would be too big for Int. Right — I need to construct a large Int64 differently. But how? Literals resolve as Int64 only where Int64 is expected. `fn wide(x: Int) -> Int64 = i64(x)` requires an Int argument. So I can't easily get a huge Int64. \n\nActually, this is a language limitation — a large Int64 literal can be written where Int64 is expected. Let me check how the existing int64 tests construct large values. Look at int64_widening or similar... Actually, since this wrap64 test isn't essential, and the wrap32 test already verifies agreement, let me skip the 64-bit wrap edge case for the smoke test (it requires language constructs that may not exist for big literals). Actually, wait — let me check: can a literal `9223372036854775807` be typed as Int64 via a return annotation? From int64_div_rem, `fn big() -> Int64 = 600851475143` — yes! A literal in a function with Int64 return type resolves as Int64. So:\n```\nfn big() -> Int64 = 9223372036854775807\nfn one() -> Int64 = 1\nsum([big(), one()])\n```\nLet me try that.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05445dddd001ieF7y5fJxdvu6w","sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","messageID":"msg_05445c4f9001lX1LkxH4ZcoAWd"}}
