ed\" for a defined function is a\n            // lie. This is where `f -1` lands: `-` cannot start a bare argument, so the parse\n            // is subtraction and `f` arrives here alone.\n            if ctx.sigs.contains_key(name) || BUILTIN_NAMES.contains(&name.as_str()) {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    *span,\n                    format!(\"`{name}` is a function, not a value; write `{name}(...)` to call it\"),\n                ));\n            }\n            Err(Error::new(*span, format!(\"`{name}` is not defined\")))\n        }\n\n        Expr::RecordLit { fields, .. } => {\n            let mut built: Vec<(String, Tir)> = Vec::new();\n            for (name, name_span, value) in fields {\n                if built.iter().any(|(seen, _)| seen == name) {\n                    return Err(Error::new(\n                        *name_span,\n                        format!(\"field `{name}` is given twice\"),","lineStart":1320,"lineEnd":1349,"totalLines":2828,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/check/mod.rs","time":{"start":1788120281277,"end":1788120281286}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The 64-bit wrap differs on jq: jq gives `9223372036854776000` (a double approximation) while the others give `-9223372036854775808` (the wrap). This is the documented jq Int64 limitation — \"exact only within +/-2^53\". From the module comment in emit_jq.rs: \"a program whose Int64 values stay within 2^53 -- every corpus case does -- agrees with the other six backends; one that leaves it diverges here, and docs/reference/types/int64.md says so.\"\n\nSo this is expected and documented behavior for jq. I should NOT add a corpus test that overflows 2^53 for Int64, since it would make backends disagree. My corpus tests must keep Int64 values within 2^53. Good — the wide smoke test I did earlier (small values) agrees everywhere.\n\nNow let me write the corpus cases and docs. Then run the full check.\n\nLet me also make sure the `just fmt` (formatter over .toy files) doesn't need the new docs. The docs `.md` aren't .toy files, so fmt doesn't touch them. But the docs fragments must compile and run correctly (docs mega-test, skipped in `just check` but run in `just test`).\n\nLet me now write the corpus cases:\n1. `sum_ints.yaml`: sum([1, 2, 3, 4]) → 10\n2. `sum_negative.yaml`: sum([1, -2, 3]) → 2\n3. `sum_int64s.yaml`: sum over Int64 → 6\n4. `sum_empty.yaml`: fn nothing() -> Vec<Int> = []; sum(nothing()) → 0\n5. `sum_wrap.yaml`: sum([2147483647, 1]) → -2147483648\n6. `max_ints.yaml`: max([3, 1, 4, 1, 5]) → 5\n7. `max_negative.yaml`: max([-5, -1, -3]) → -1\n8. `max_int64s.yaml`: max over Int64 → 9\n9. `max_empty.yaml`: fn nothing() -> Vec<Int> = []; max(nothing()) → null\n10. `max_empty_unwrap_refuses.yaml`: max(nothing())! → refuses\n\nAnd checker error tests in a new test file or existing. Since sum/max are builtins, I could add tests to a new `tests/sum_max.rs`? Hmm, is there a pattern for builtin compile errors? Let me check `tests/jsonlines.rs` since jsonlines is also polymorphic.\n\nActually, let me look at how the reserved-name test works and where builtin-related compile errors live. There's `tests/unwrap.rs` with `a_builtin_cannot_be_redefined` and `str_takes_an_int`. Let me add a `tests/reductions.rs` file with:\n- sum needs a Vec of Int or Int64, found Int\n- sum needs a Vec of Int or Int64, found Vec<Str>\n- max needs a Vec of Int or Int64, found Int\n- max needs a Vec of Int or Int64, found Vec<{n: Int}>\n- sum cannot be redefined (reserved name)\n\nLet me check the reserved-name mechanism: check/mod.rs:1332 uses BUILTIN_NAMES. Since I added sum/max to BUILTIN_NAMES, a program defining `fn sum(...)` should be refused. Let me verify the message for redefining builtins. Let me look at line 1332.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_054462f7d001D0Yf9p6Ml5toeQ","sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","messageID":"msg_05445ed41001WivFaFJDnIxSro"}}
