{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788120308994,"sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a3be9d2e67150280","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/tests/corpus.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/tests/corpus.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The agreement harness.\n2: //!\n3: //! One corpus, every backend. This is the check a single-backend suite cannot express: two\n4: //! backends producing different answers is a failure in its own right, separate from either of\n5: //! them producing a wrong one. The step_*.rs suites keep their own job, which is pinning error\n6: //! messages and emitted code; this is about behaviour, and behaviour is what has to be identical\n7: //! across targets.\n8: \n9: mod support;\n10: \n11: use toylang::Backend;\n12: \n13: #[test]\n14: fn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right() {\n15:     let cases = support::cases();\n16:     assert!(\n17:         !cases.is_empty(),\n18:         \"the corpus is empty, so this test proves nothing\"\n19:     );\n20: \n21:     let mut failures = Vec::new();\n22: \n23:     for case in &cases {\n24:         failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n25:             &case.name,\n26:             &case.program,\n27:             case.input.as_deref(),\n28:             &case.expect,\n29:         ));\n30:     }\n31: \n32:     assert!(\n33:         failures.is_empty(),\n34:         \"{} of {} corpus programs failed across {} backends:\\n{}\",\n35:         failures.len(),\n36:         cases.len(),\n37:         Backend::ALL.len(),\n38:         failures.join(\"\\n\")\n39:     );\n40: }\n41: \n42: /// What a program prints is not always the whole claim. A case that asks for it gets the code a\n43: /// backend emitted pinned as well, which is how a property invisible in the output -- a name\n44: /// that has to be declared, a type that has to be spelled -- stays observed.\n45: #[test]\n46: fn emitted_code_matches_the_snapshot() {\n47:     let mut asked = 0;\n48:     for case in support::cases() {\n49:         let program =\n50:             toylang::compile(&case.program).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"{}: {e}\", case.name));\n51:         for backend in case.snapshot {\n52:             let emitted = backend.emit(&program).unwrap_or_else(|e| {\n53:                 panic!(\"{}: {} could not emit: {e}\", case.name, backend.name())\n54:             });\n55:             insta::assert_snapshot!(format!(\"{}__{}\", case.name, backend.name()), emitted);\n56:             asked += 1;\n57:         }\n58:     }\n59:     assert!(\n60:         asked > 0,\n61:         \"no case asks for a snapshot, so this test proves nothing\"\n62:     );\n63: }\n\n(End of file - total 63 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The agreement harness.\n//!\n//! One corpus, every backend. This is the check a single-backend suite cannot express: two\n//! backends producing different answers is a failure in its own right, separate from either of\n//! them producing a wrong one. The step_*.rs suites keep their own job, which is pinning error\n//! messages and emitted code; this is about behaviour, and behaviour is what has to be identical\n//! across targets.\n\nmod support;\n\nuse toylang::Backend;\n\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right() {\n    let cases = support::cases();\n    assert!(\n        !cases.is_empty(),\n        \"the corpus is empty, so this test proves nothing\"\n    );\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/tests/corpus.rs","text":"//! The agreement harness.\n//!\n//! One corpus, every backend. This is the check a single-backend suite cannot express: two\n//! backends producing different answers is a failure in its own right, separate from either of\n//! them producing a wrong one. The step_*.rs suites keep their own job, which is pinning error\n//! messages and emitted code; this is about behaviour, and behaviour is what has to be identical\n//! across targets.\n\nmod support;\n\nuse toylang::Backend;\n\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right() {\n    let cases = support::cases();\n    assert!(\n        !cases.is_empty(),\n        \"the corpus is empty, so this test proves nothing\"\n    );\n\n    let mut failures = Vec::new();\n\n    for case in &cases {\n        failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n            &case.name,\n            &case.program,\n            case.input.as_deref(),\n            &case.expect,\n        ));\n    }\n\n    assert!(\n        failures.is_empty(),\n        \"{} of {} corpus programs failed across {} backends:\\n{}\",\n        failures.len(),\n        cases.len(),\n        Backend::ALL.len(),\n        failures.join(\"\\n\")\n    );\n}\n\n/// What a program prints is not always the whole claim. A case that asks for it gets the code a\n/// backend emitted pinned as well, which is how a property invisible in the output -- a name\n/// that has to be declared, a type that has to be spelled -- stays observed.\n#[test]\nfn emitted_code_matches_the_snapshot() {\n    let mut asked = 0;\n    for case in support::cases() {\n        let program =\n            toylang::compile(&case.program).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"{}: {e}\", case.name));\n        for backend in case.snapshot {\n            let emitted = backend.emit(&program).unwrap_or_else(|e| {\n                panic!(\"{}: {} could not emit: {e}\", case.name, backend.name())\n            });\n            insta::assert_snapshot!(format!(\"{}__{}\", case.name, backend.name()), emitted);\n            asked += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    assert!(\n        asked > 0,\n        \"no case asks for a snapshot, so this test proves nothing\"\n    );\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":63,"totalLines":63,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests/corpus.rs","time":{"start":1788120308988,"end":1788120308991}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Since `sum` and `max` are in BUILTIN_NAMES, they can't be redefined. Good — no extra work needed there; I can add a test.\n\nNow let me write the corpus cases and the test file. Let me create the corpus files first using the write tool.\n\nLet me check the exact `node_types` tags I'll need — the `tag_corpus.rs` test auto-generates them on every run, so I can leave them blank or wrong; the test will fix them. But wait — the tag_corpus test patches the files on every run. So when I run `just check`, it will update node_types. But the corpus test (`tests/corpus.rs`) requires node_types to exist? Let me check — node_types is `#[serde(default)]`, so it can be absent. But there might be a check that the tags are fresh. Let me look at tests/corpus.rs.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_054469abe001HbuWwoUXaHW3RZ","sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","messageID":"msg_054466a50001fwfB1HsWpKKZ1R"}}
