{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788120408527,"sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","part":{"id":"prt_0544821c5001I565K5GHJJVnUJ","messageID":"msg_054481329001f41IpdNzQioerv","sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","snapshot":"c53e00b794036d1f0787951a383134d012ed22a8","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788120446387,"sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-87b5cd4b2bf48cff","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"//! What the checker refuses about `sum` and `max` (kantord/toylang#140).\n\n/// `sum` is defined only for the two integer element types, so a scalar argument is refused\n/// with the restricted set named, the way `sort` names its own.\n#[test]\nfn sum_takes_a_vec() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(r#\"sum(1)\"#)\n            .map(|_| ())\n            .unwrap_err()\n            .to_string()\n    );\n}\n\n/// Neither Str nor Char reduces (the ruling cut them on the same no-caller grounds it cut min\n/// and product), so a Vec of either is refused rather than silently ordered.\n#[test]\nfn sum_takes_an_int_element() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(r#\"sum([\"a\"])\"#)\n            .map(|_| ())\n            .unwrap_err()\n            .to_string()\n    );\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn max_takes_a_vec() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(r#\"max(1)\"#)\n            .map(|_| ())\n            .unwrap_err()\n            .to_string()\n    );\n}\n\n/// A record has no total order, so a Vec of records cannot be reduced to a maximum.\n#[test]\nfn max_takes_an_int_element() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(r#\"max([{n: 1}])\"#)\n            .map(|_| ())\n            .unwrap_err()\n            .to_string()\n    );\n}\n\n/// A builtin is a reserved name: a program that defines `sum` and means something else by it\n/// is refused the same way `str` is, rather than silently shadowed.\n#[test]\nfn a_builtin_cannot_be_redefined() {\n    insta::assert_snapshot!(\n        toylang::compile(\"fn sum(x: Vec<Int>) -> Int = x[0]!\\n\\nsum([1])\")\n            .map(|_| ())\n            .unwrap_err()\n            .to_string()\n    );\n}\n","filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/tests/reductions.rs"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-140/tests/reductions.rs","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"tests/reductions.rs","time":{"start":1788120446383,"end":1788120446386}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me create the checker-error tests. I'll make a new test file `tests/reductions.rs`. Let me follow the pattern of `tests/unwrap.rs` with `insta::assert_snapshot!`.\n\nTests:\n1. `sum_takes_a_vec_of_ints`: `sum(1)` → \"`sum` needs a Vec of Int or Int64, found Int (at byte 0)\"\n2. `sum_takes_an_int_element`: `sum([\"a\"])` → \"`sum` needs a Vec of Int or Int64, found Vec<Str> (at byte 0)\"\n3. `max_takes_a_vec_of_ints`: `max(1)` → \"`max` needs a Vec of Int or Int64, found Int (at byte 0)\"\n4. `max_takes_an_int_element`: `max([{n: 1}])` → \"`max` needs a Vec of Int or Int64, found Vec<{n: Int}> (at byte 0)\"\n5. `a_builtin_cannot_be_redefined` for sum: `fn sum(x: Vec<Int>) -> Int = x[0]!` → \"`sum` is a builtin and cannot be redefined (at byte 0)\"\n6. max over an empty vec of records? No — max of `[{n:1}]` covers the record case.\n\nWait, let me double check error spans. In `sort([{n: 1}])` docs example, the error is \"at byte 5\" (the `[` of the arg). For `sum([\"a\"])`, the arg is `[\"a\"]`, so byte 0. For `sum(1)`, arg `1` at byte 0.\n\nActually let me just write the tests and generate snapshots with INSTA_UPDATE=always, then verify the snapshot content.\n\nLet me also double-check: what's the display of `Vec<Str>`? `Vec<Str>`. And `Vec<{n: Int}>` displays as `Vec<{n: Int}>`. Good.\n\nLet me write the test file.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_0544884370016lHLsi47xWSYUs","sessionID":"ses_fabcc0d67ffe81ceFkgf1oLPKJ","messageID":"msg_054481329001f41IpdNzQioerv"}}
